      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of days on which the number of bacteria exceeds 100. We start with the initial number of bacteria and observe how the number doubles each day.\n\n1. **Initial number of bacteria:**\n   \\[\n   B_0 = 3\n   \\]\n\n2. **Number of bacteria at the end of day 1:**\n   \\[\n   B_1 = 2 \\times B_0 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n   \\]\n\n3. **Number of bacteria at the end of day 2:**\n   \\[\n   B_2 = 2 \\times B_1 = 2 \\times 6 = 12\n   \\]\n\n4. **Number of bacteria at the end of day 3:**\n   \\[\n   B_3 = 2 \\times B_2 = 2 \\times 12 = 24\n   \\]\n\n5. **Number of bacteria at the end of day 4:**\n   \\[\n   B_4 = 2 \\times B_3 = 2 \\times 24 = 48\n   \\]\n\n6. **Number of bacteria at the end of day 5:**\n   \\[\n   B_5 = 2 \\times B_4 = 2 \\times 48 = 96\n   \\]\n\n7. **Number of bacteria at the end of day 6:**\n   \\[\n   B_6 = 2 \\times B_5 = 2 \\times 96 = 192\n   \\]\n\nNow, we compare the number of bacteria at the end of each day to find the first day on which the colony has more than 100 bacteria:\n- Day 5: 96\n- Day 6: 192\n\nWe see that the number of bacteria just exceeds 100 on day 6.\n\nThus, the number of the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
