      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of days \\( n \\) on which the colony of bacteria first exceeds 100 bacteria, given that the colony starts with 3 bacteria and doubles in size every day. We can express the number of bacteria at the end of day \\( n \\) as:\n\n\\[ \\text{Number of bacteria at the end of day } n = 3 \\times 2^n \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest integer \\( n \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^n > 100 \\]\n\nFirst, we divide both sides of the inequality by 3:\n\n\\[ 2^n > \\frac{100}{3} \\]\n\nCalculating the right-hand side:\n\n\\[ \\frac{100}{3} \\approx 33.33 \\]\n\nSo, we need:\n\n\\[ 2^n > 33.33 \\]\n\nNext, we find the smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies this inequality. We can do this by evaluating powers of 2:\n\n\\[ 2^5 = 32 \\]\n\\[ 2^6 = 64 \\]\n\nSince \\( 2^5 = 32 \\) is less than 33.33 and \\( 2^6 = 64 \\) is greater than 33.33, the smallest integer \\( n \\) that satisfies the inequality is \\( n = 6 \\).\n\nTo confirm, we check the number of bacteria at the end of day 6:\n\n\\[ 3 \\times 2^6 = 3 \\times 64 = 192 \\]\n\nSince 192 is greater than 100, the number of the first day which ends with the colony having more than 100 bacteria is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{6} \\]",
